<p>A puzzle by Henry Dudeney, from 1931:</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/2026-03-20-square-diamonds-1.png" alt="https://archive.org/details/the-strand-magazine-v-082n-488-1931-08-fc-ifc-ads-ibc-bc-beb/page/n111/mode/2up?q=perplexities" width="327" height="428" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-72952" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/2026-03-20-square-diamonds-1.png 327w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/2026-03-20-square-diamonds-1-300x393.png 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 327px) 100vw, 327px" /></p>
<p>&#8220;Here you have the two to the ten of Diamonds, inclusive. Can you re-arrange them, still retaining the shape of the figure, so that the &#8216;pips&#8217; total eighteen, across and down, in each line, and also diagonally from the corners?&#8221;</p>

